  <data name="Cli_Help_Main" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed &lt;command&gt; [arguments] [options]
       hexed &lt;file&gt; [options]          (same as: hexed open &lt;file&gt;)

Commands:
  open      Open files, disks or processes in the HexEditor window
  hash      Compute and verify hashes and checksums
  dump      Print a hex dump
  compare   Compare two files
  run       Run a script without a window
  template  Apply a structure template and print the result
  patch     Apply an IPS patch to a file
  convert   Convert between formats (Intel HEX, S-record, Base64, source code, ...)
  find      Search files and print the matches

Common options (all commands):
  -h, --help           Show the usage (also: hexed &lt;command&gt; --help)
      --version        Show the version
  -q, --quiet          Print nothing but the result
      --format &lt;f&gt;     Output format: text (default) or json
  -o, --output &lt;path&gt;  Write the result to a file instead of the screen
      --lang &lt;tag&gt;     Language of the messages (for example en, ja)
      --no-color       Do not use colors
      --unregister     Remove the registrations made on this PC (context menu, file
                       associations, jump list, PATH, temporary folder)

Exit codes: 0 success, 1 negative result (differences, no match, mismatch), 2 usage error,
3 I/O error, 4 script or template error, 5 not permitted, 6 cancelled, 7 limit exceeded,
8 format error, 10 internal error.

Offsets, lengths and ranges accept expressions. A number without a prefix is decimal; use
0x for hexadecimal. A range is start:length or start..end (end is not included); 'end' is
the file length (for example end-16:16). In PowerShell, put values that contain $ in single
quotes (for example '$1F00').</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_open" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed open [paths...] [options]

Opens files in the HexEditor window. If HexEditor is already running, the files open as tabs
there. Relative paths are resolved from the current folder.

Options:
  -g, --offset &lt;expr&gt;      Put the cursor at this position (for example 0x1000 or end-16)
      --select &lt;range&gt;     Select this range (start:length or start..end)
  -r, --readonly           Open read-only
      --disk &lt;n|path&gt;      Open a physical disk (--disk 0, --disk \\.\PhysicalDrive1)
      --volume &lt;drive&gt;     Open a volume (--volume C:)
      --process &lt;pid|name&gt; Open the memory of a process
      --encoding &lt;name&gt;    Character encoding of the text column
      --template &lt;name|path&gt;  Apply a structure template after opening
      --compare &lt;a&gt; &lt;b&gt;    Open two files in the compare view
      --run &lt;script&gt;       Run a script after opening
      --new-window         Open in a new window
      --new-document       Open a new empty document
      --open-disk          Show the Open Disk dialog
      --new-instance       Start a separate process instead of using the running one
  -w, --wait               Do not return until the opened documents are closed
      --safe-mode          Start in safe mode (new process only)
      --write-block        Start with write protection for the whole app
      --pseudo-locale &lt;qps-ploc|qps-plocm&gt;   Show pseudo-translated text (preview builds)
      --plugin-dev &lt;folder&gt; Load a plugin under development (new process only)
      --ui-lang &lt;tag&gt;      Display language of the window (new process only)
      --test-hooks &lt;file&gt;  Enable fault injection (test builds only)

In PowerShell, put values that contain $ in single quotes (for example '$1F00').</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_hash" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed hash [paths...] [options]
       hexed hash --check &lt;checksum file&gt; [options]

Computes hashes and checksums. Paths may contain wildcards (*.bin) or be folders; use - to read
standard input. Several algorithms are computed in one pass.

Options:
  -a, --algorithm &lt;id,...&gt;   Algorithm IDs, comma separated (default sha256).
                             '--algorithm list' prints the available IDs
      --range &lt;range&gt;        Hash only this range (start:length or start..end)
      --format &lt;f&gt;           text (default): algorithm  value  path
                             gnu: value  path (the same as sha256sum)
                             bsd: SHA256 (path) = value
                             json: one object per file
      --check &lt;file&gt;         Verify the files listed in a checksum file (.sha256, .md5,
                             SHA256SUMS, .sfv, ...) and print OK, FAILED or MISSING
  -r, --recurse              Include subfolders when a folder is given

Exit codes: 0 success, 1 a mismatch with --check, 3 a file could not be read.
In PowerShell, put values that contain $ in single quotes (for example '$1F00').</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_dump" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed dump &lt;path&gt; [options]

Prints a hex dump of a file (or a range) to standard output. Memory use does not depend on the size.

Options:
      --range &lt;range&gt;        Range to print (start:length or start..end; default: the whole file)
      --width &lt;n&gt;            Bytes per line (default 16)
      --group &lt;n&gt;            Bytes per group (default depends on the style; 0 for none)
      --encoding &lt;name&gt;      Character encoding of the text column (default ascii)
      --base-address &lt;expr&gt;  Address shown for the first byte (default 0)
      --style &lt;style&gt;        default (offset, hex, |text|), xxd, hexdump-c, plain,
                             c, cpp, csharp, java, js, python, rust, go, pascal, vbnet,
                             base64, base32, ascii85, uu, qp, hextext, dectext
      --squeeze              Print * for lines that repeat the previous line (like hexdump -C)

Exit codes: 2 if the range is outside the file.
In PowerShell, put values that contain $ in single quotes (for example '$1F00').</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_compare" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed compare &lt;path1&gt; &lt;path2&gt; [options]

Compares two files and prints the differences.

Options:
      --mode &lt;byte|resync&gt;   byte (default): compare bytes at the same offsets
                             resync: allow insertions and deletions
      --range1 &lt;range&gt;       Range of the first file (default: all)
      --range2 &lt;range&gt;       Range of the second file (default: all)
      --max &lt;n&gt;              Maximum number of differences to print (default 1000);
                             the rest is only counted
      --format &lt;text|json&gt;   Output format
      --brief                Print one line (same or different) and stop at the first difference

Exit codes: 0 the same, 1 different, 3 a file could not be read.
In PowerShell, put values that contain $ in single quotes (for example '$1F00').</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_run" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed run &lt;script&gt; [files...] [options]

Runs a script without opening a window. The files are opened in order (app.documents); the first
one is app.activeDocument.

Options:
      --arg &lt;name=value&gt;   Script parameter (repeatable); also available in script.args
      --trust              Run an untrusted script without asking
      --allow &lt;perm,...&gt;   Permissions to grant (for example file-read)
      --save               Save every changed document when the script ends
      --save-as &lt;path&gt;     Save the first document to this path (the original is not changed)
      --dry-run            Save nothing; print a summary of the changes
      --timeout &lt;seconds&gt;  Maximum run time of the whole script
  -r, --readonly           Open the files read-only

By default only documents for which the script calls doc.save() are saved.
The exit code of a script can be set with app.exit(code). Codes 2 to 10 have the meanings listed
in 'hexed --help'; use 64 or more for your own meanings.</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_template" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed template &lt;path&gt; (--template &lt;name|path&gt; | --auto) [options]

Applies a structure template to a file and prints the result.

Options:
      --template &lt;name|path&gt;  Template to apply
      --auto                  Choose the template automatically (exit code 1 if none fits)
  -g, --offset &lt;expr&gt;         Start offset of the structure (default 0)
      --format &lt;f&gt;            json, csv or text (an indented tree; default text)
      --depth &lt;n&gt;             Maximum depth of the tree
      --fields &lt;path,...&gt;     Print only these fields (for example header.e_lfanew,sections[*].name)

Exit codes: 4 for a template error.
In PowerShell, put values that contain $ in single quotes (for example '$1F00').</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_patch" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed patch &lt;target&gt; &lt;patch&gt; (-o &lt;output&gt; | --in-place) [options]

Applies a patch file to a file. One of -o and --in-place is required. This version applies IPS
and IPS32 patches.

Options:
  -o, --output &lt;path&gt;       Write the patched file here (the target is not changed)
      --in-place            Replace the target safely (a temporary file is written first)
      --format &lt;f&gt;          auto (default), ips or ips32
      --verify-before &lt;a:v&gt; Check the hash of the target first (for example sha256:ab12...);
                            exit code 1 and nothing is written if it differs
      --verify-after &lt;a:v&gt;  Check the hash of the result before writing it
      --dry-run             Print the number of records and changed bytes; apply nothing

Exit codes: 1 verification failed, 8 the patch is broken.</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_convert" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed convert &lt;input&gt; &lt;output&gt; [options]

Converts a file between the import/export formats of HexEditor.

Options:
      --from &lt;format&gt;       Input format (default: detected from the content and extension)
      --to &lt;format&gt;         Output format (default: from the output extension; bin if unknown)
                            bin, ihex, srec, base64, base32, ascii85, uu, qp, url, hextext,
                            dectext, c, cpp, csharp, java, js, python, rust, go, pascal, vbnet
      --ignore-checksum     Read Intel HEX / S-record files even if a checksum is wrong
      --fill &lt;hex&gt;          Byte used for gaps (default FF)
      --address-mode &lt;m&gt;    Intel HEX: auto, linear, segment, i8, i16, i32; S-record: auto, s1, s2, s3

Format options have the names of the fields of the import/export dialogs, written in
lowercase with hyphens, for example --record-length 32, --start-address 0x08000000,
--bytes-per-line 16, --line-length 64, --url-safe, --no-upper-case, --new-line lf,
--encoding utf8.

Exit codes: 8 for a checksum or format error in the input.</value></data>

  <data name="Cli_Help_find" xml:space="preserve"><value>Usage: hexed find &lt;paths...&gt; (--hex &lt;pattern&gt; | --text &lt;string&gt; | --regex &lt;expr&gt;) [options]

Searches files and prints "path  offset  length" for each match.

Options:
      --hex &lt;pattern&gt;      Bytes, for example "DE AD ?? EF" (?? is any byte, ? any nibble)
      --text &lt;string&gt;      Text
      --regex &lt;expr&gt;       Regular expression (on text)
      --encoding &lt;name&gt;    Character encoding of --text and --regex (default utf-8)
      --case-sensitive     Match upper and lower case differently
      --range &lt;range&gt;      Search only this range of each file
      --max &lt;n&gt;            Stop after n matches
      --format &lt;text|json&gt; Output format
  -r, --recurse            Include subfolders when a folder is given

Exit codes: 0 found, 1 not found, 3 a file could not be read.
In PowerShell, put values that contain $ in single quotes (for example '$1F00').</value></data>

